{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>For these nations, etc. — will hear.</b> Meaning, they have [only] to hear and accept what the diviners tell them, for the diviners make known to them what is inscribed in the stars. <b>But as for you — not so:</b> meaning, the knowledge of future events shall not be for you thus, but rather ״A prophet I will raise up,״ etc." "<b>Or</b> it may allude in this manner: ״<b>Not so</b>״, etc. — meaning, you are not in this condition like them; rather, HaShem your Elohim has placed under your dominion all the constellations and all the host of heaven, as it says (Psalms 8:7): ״כֹּל שַׁתָּה תַחַת רַגְלָיו,״ and as they of blessed memory expounded (Shabbat 156a) on the verse ״צֶדֶק יִקְרָאֵהוּ לְרַגְלוֹ״ (Isaiah 41:2)." "And its saying <b>A prophet I will raise up</b>, etc., comes to answer: after all, according to this the nations would be at a great level, that they know future events — which Israel does not. Therefore it says ״A prophet I will raise up,״ etc. And perhaps this is what Saul understood when he inquired of the woman with the ov (I Samuel 28:8) — for HaShem did not forbid inquiring of an ov or yid'oni except in a place where He has established a prophet; but other than this, if they need to know something, they may inquire through them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {1 "The Talmudic citation is given as שבת קנו: i.e., Shabbat 156a-b; the closing quote mark was normalized to a colon per the system's convention for folio references."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:04.935728540Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}